<worksheet xml:id="sec-quiz-one-proportion-ht-att-2">
  <title>Quiz: One-Proportion Hypothesis Tests Attempt 2</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Name: <fillin characters="45"/></p>

    <assemblage>
      <p><em>One-proportion hypothesis test formulas and R commands</em></p>
      <p>
        <m>\widehat{p}=\dfrac{x}{n}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>z=\dfrac{\widehat{p}-p_0}{\sqrt{\dfrac{p_0(1-p_0)}{n}}}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>P(Z\le z)=\texttt{pnorm(z)}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>P(Z&gt;z)=\texttt{pnorm(z,lower.tail=FALSE)}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Two-tailed p-value: <m>2\cdot\texttt{pnorm(-abs(z))}</m>.
        For critical values, <c>qnorm(q)</c> returns the standard normal
        value with cumulative probability <m>q</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>p_0</m> is the null-hypothesis reference proportion.
        Normal-approximation conditions:
        <m>np_0\ge 10</m> and <m>n(1-p_0)\ge 10</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Complete a one-proportion <m>z</m> test for each problem. Define the
      population proportion; state <m>H_0</m> and <m>H_a</m> and identify
      which contains the claim; state the significance level and test
      direction; check the normal-approximation conditions; calculate
      <m>\widehat{p}</m> and <m>z</m>; find the p-value or critical value(s);
      state your decision about <m>H_0</m>; and write a conclusion about
      the original claim in context.
    </p>
    <p>
      Assume random sampling and independent observations for these
      exercises. Show your work or R commands. Keep full precision during
      calculations; round test statistics and critical values to three
      decimal places and p-values to four decimal places.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <!-- Source: 1propHT.docx, Question 4; original data, claim, and significance level. -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ht-att-2-zika" workspace="2.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
        A researcher claims that at least 83% of Americans believe they are
        unlikely to contract the Zika virus. In a random sample of 215
        Americans, 176 say they are unlikely to contract the virus. Test the
        researcher's claim using a significance level of <m>0.08</m>.
        Is there sufficient evidence to reject the claim?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
    <!-- Source: 1propHT.docx, Question 3; original data, claim, and significance level; placebo rate treated as a fixed reference. -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ht-att-2-tamiflu" workspace="5.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
        In a clinical trial, 63 of the 708 flu patients treated with Tamiflu
        (oseltamivir phosphate) experienced nausea. Test the claim that the
        nausea rate for Tamiflu users is greater than the 6% rate reported
        for patients given a placebo. Treat 6% as a fixed reference value
        and use a significance level of <m>0.09</m>. Is there sufficient
        evidence to support the claim?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
